\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 The hover-copt, sleek and luxurious, lifted from the highest pad, yawed and tracked west. Half an hour hence, with the setting sun\'92s rays pouring through the windows and warming the leather seats, it descended into dusk towards a ground pad. Painted across its landing circle, blocky white words were easily read from hundreds of metres away, and told Sarah, Kathy and Hayley where they were going: Sublime Point. Eloise\'92s residential compound. Probably one of the safest locations in the country. In the muddling shadows, the compound appeared to be the only buildings in tens of square kilometres of blue-green native bushland on a plateau above the Layer, reputably restored by Eloise to their pre-colonialist condition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I could work from here,\'92 Hayley muttered. Kathy and Sarah exchanged glances, fearing Sarah had exchanged one prison for another, one in which the other two could be sentenced as her inmates.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise seemed to be dozing, and opened her eyes only as the copter began descending. She took a long, restorative breath and started working on her tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the hover-copt approached, Sarah saw amorphous glassy flashes of light twinkling from a flat-ish, open, rocky space surrounded by a larger pentagonal organically laid-out bush garden. As it descended, her mind wrestled with rendering her perception into three-dimensional images she could understand. She gasped as, out the rocky space, a building seemed to leap and resolve into a multi-storey triumph of materials science and complex mathematics, a palace of such modernity and yet reminiscently medieval\'97a kind of Cinderella Castle imbued with Islamic sensibilities at their peak of astronomy and poetry. It was constructed of interweaving triangles of sweeping, almost four-dimensional curves of bush-smoked glass with such artistry it was unclear how the pieces were held together.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked again and thought she saw four\'97no, five\'97giant, straight trees growing right out of the building. Their position formed a perfect pentagon. As the hover-copt drew nearer, the trees flickered like failing holograms and became tall \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 thin minarets fashioned by lattice of  metal that spiralled all the way up to their tapering points.\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt landed; a waiting ground car rolled to within metres. The passengers alighted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the ground, the palace appeared less imposing than from the air. Closer up, Sarah could work out more of how it was constructed; which, if anything, made it more wondrous, held together impossibly wrought slender tendrils of advanced metal alloys. It would not not out of place on the cover of a futuristic sci-fi fantasy. An advanced civilisation, Sarah mused, who conquered the universe by shaming other worlds into submission for their inadequate feats of architecture.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Two uniformed guard-type women blocked Sarah and the others when they starting following Eloise towards the palace. \'91Please accompany us,\'92 one said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise turned. \'91It\'92s OK,\'92 she reassured. \'91They\'92ll get you settled in for the evening.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy and Hayley squared off against Eloise\'92s girl goons. \'91Are we free to leave if we wish?\'92 Hayley asked, sounding like her whole existence was made for conducting investigations.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise threw her hands out, and encompassing, inclusive gesture. \'91Of course. But I think you\'92ll all agree, given today\'92s events, you\'92re safest here. Stay the night. You\'92ll be accommodated in luxury, I assure you. We\'92ll talk in the morning, and assess what you want to do next.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s all right,\'92 Sarah placated. Perhaps it was no more than Airborne confirmation bias, but if she was still wary of Eloise, she trusted 
\f2\i this place
\f1\i0 , with all its statements of wealth and power.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy and Hayley exchanged looks of shared suspicion. Kathy gestured to Sarah: 
\f2\i I go where she goes
\f1\i0 . Hayley stiffened and watched the pair turn for the second car. She relented and followed, a few steps behind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The trip in the car was short; only a few hundred metres. In the failing light Sarah lost her sense of direction as the road wound around stands of native bushland, and a small village of buildings. Earlier, from the air, Sarah saw an array of more conventional buildings that reminded her of a stately university campus. From the car, she saw many more than could be seen from the air; grey one-storey buildings nestled under canopies of towering eucalypts and camouflaged by the materials and shapes of their roofing. Sarah wondered about the widow-maker branches that would inevitably drop from the trees; the buildings must be sturdy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The car turned onto a roundabout and circled around\'97to the south, Sarah thought\'97a clutch of three-storey dwellings that reached out towards the road like splayed fingers. The ground car stopped outside the third.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Come on,\'92 the driver said as she got out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They walked a curve path towards an inviting portico, subtly lit with warm lighting, which intensified the nearer they approached. At the front door, the driver waved her hand over a panel in the exterior brick. The door clicked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ll be staying here,\'92 the driver said to Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah eye\'92s quizzed for more explanation. \'91You have the next townhouse,\'92 the driver said to Kathy and Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you locking us in?\'92 Hayley asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No. But be careful you don\'92t inadvertently lock yourself outside.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah found the way the driver said 
\f2\i inadvertently
\f1\i0  disconcerting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92ll sort out proper access in the morning. For now, please enjoy the hospitality of Eloise Shakti. I\'92m sure you find every comfort and luxury you could possibly want for the evening inside.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy reached for Sarah\'92s elbow. \'91It\'92s all right. We\'92ll see you in the morning.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded and went inside.\
